00:43:14.240 --> 00:43:19.360
table from our other construct, but we didn't&nbsp;
expose that. So let's go back to our construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:39.840
add the table property and assign&nbsp;
the table to that property.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:53.840
With that, we can pass the hello with counter dot&nbsp;
table property into our third party construct.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.120 --> 00:44:01.280
Now if we deploy and skip ahead, we'll see&nbsp;
that the table viewer construct created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:05.520
separate API gateway and its own&nbsp;
endpoint defined in the outputs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.400 --> 00:44:11.520
If we go there, it will end up&nbsp;
displaying what's in the DynamoDB table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:23.440
Testing constructs is one of the&nbsp;
most powerful things about CDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:29.760
The CDK workshop walks you through two types&nbsp;
of tests, assertion tests and validation tests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:34.560
For the first assertion test,&nbsp;
we're going to create a stack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:39.920
and use our hit counter construct and then&nbsp;
verify that one DynamoDB table was created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:54.480
So let's go back to the code. Create&nbsp;
the test and remove the old ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:03.840
Now we can run it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:28.080
And one DynamoDB table was created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:35.520
Next we'll move on to checking the lambda. The&nbsp;
CDK assertions library has some useful features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:39.920
like capture, which can intercept different&nbsp;
properties as part of the template synthesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:44.320
In this case, we use CAPTCHA to verify&nbsp;
that the correct downstream function and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:50.320
table names are being passed into the hit&nbsp;
counter lambda. When we run this test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.320 --> 00:45:54.560
we expect it to fail because I didn't copy&nbsp;
the references from the synthesized output.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.560 --> 00:46:01.840
But making it fail actually makes it easier&nbsp;
to find them. So we'll grab the correct names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.680 --> 00:46:13.840
and update the test with the right ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:29.040
Now when we run it, it'll pass. Next, let's switch&nbsp;
to a test driven development mindset. And say we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:33.440
want to make sure our table is using server&nbsp;
side encryption, we can assert that DynamoDB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:41.840
table resource has server side encryption&nbsp;
enabled. So when we run the test, it'll fail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:51.920
From a development perspective, that&nbsp;
means we need to go back to the construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.400 --> 00:46:56.880
and make sure that server&nbsp;
side encryption is enabled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:15.120
Now if we run the test again, it'll&nbsp;
pass. Since we don't actually want that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:18.720
I'm going to remove it from the&nbsp;
construct and remove the test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.680 --> 00:47:28.800
Next, let's talk about validation testing.&nbsp;
Let's say as part of your construct, you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:32.640
to make sure a sane number of read capacity&nbsp;
units are being used in the DynamoDB table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:42.960
We can add a read capacity property that gets&nbsp;
passed into the construct. Use it in the table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:51.360
And then in the constructor, we can&nbsp;
validate that the value passed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:56.160
is reasonable, say between five and&nbsp;
20. If it's not, we'll throw an error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:07.440
Now let's add a test that passes in a value out&nbsp;
of range to a three and verify that the error&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:14.960
is thrown. When we run the test, it'll&nbsp;
pass because the error is being thrown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:21.280
This error would be thrown as part&nbsp;
of stack synthesis. So let's see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:24.320
we'll go back to our workshop stack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:33.840
Add an out of bounds read capacity&nbsp;
value and run the stack synthesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:43.600
As you can see there is validation&nbsp;
testing is super useful. At my work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:45.840
I use it to help enforce some naming conventions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:50.480
and also to log or throw deprecation warnings&nbsp;
when property inputs or defaults change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:57.280
Great job with the workshop.&nbsp;
Now that the workshops done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.280 --> 00:49:01.840
we can move on to some advanced topics&nbsp;
like aspects and best practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:07.360
In the workshop, we went over fine grained&nbsp;
assertions by checking for things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:13.520
number of tables created and ensuring encryption&nbsp;
was turned on. We're checking to make sure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.520 --> 00:49:20.000
lamda environment variables being passed in or&nbsp;
the right one. But you could also use snapshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:25.600
testing to check the entire template if you want&nbsp;
to do and use gests match snapshot functionality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:32.800
We also went over validation testing as part of&nbsp;
the workshop. We checked the properties being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.800 --> 00:49:37.520
passed into the construct to make sure read&nbsp;
capacity units were within a specific range.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:44.000
In my organization, we also use this&nbsp;
to help enforce some naming conventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:48.560
as well as throw some deprecation warnings in&nbsp;
the event we have to significantly change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:53.680
construct. In this case, the warnings won't&nbsp;
block deployment, but it will show up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:53.680 --> 00:49:57.840
console when doing synthetic deploy, where&nbsp;
developers can pick them up and fix them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:59.600 --> 00:50:02.480
You are aren't limited to just&nbsp;
logging errors to the console though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:08.400
when you sent you're executing the actual&nbsp;
code. So you could also log to an external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:12.880
service like a platform service that tracks&nbsp;
deployments, or logs to cloud watch itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:22.160
The last form of testing is integration testing.&nbsp;
With CDK, you can use AWS custom resources to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.160 --> 00:50:27.200
that the resources are working together correctly&nbsp;
as part of the CloudFormation deployment. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.200 --> 00:50:30.880
something breaks, it will automatically&nbsp;
roll back the CloudFormation deployment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:40.160
CDK has a provider framework for interfacing&nbsp;
with CloudFormation custom resources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:44.560
These custom resources enable you to write&nbsp;
custom provisioning logic and templates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:50.160
CloudFormation runs anytime you create,&nbsp;
update or delete stacks. We can make use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:54.640
of this to do integration testing across&nbsp;
our stack. Let's say you have an event bus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:59.120
a lamda. And a DynamoDB table that you want&nbsp;
to make sure are interacting with each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:04.720
You could use the provider framework to spin up&nbsp;
a lambda to emit a test event on an Event Bus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:05.440 --> 00:51:11.120
all part of the CloudFormation deployment. And&nbsp;
then the custom resource can query DynamoDB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.120 --> 00:51:15.360
waiting for the change. If the change&nbsp;
doesn't happen in a set amount of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:21.840
the build will fail and CloudFormation&nbsp;
will automatically roll back the changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:28.320
Matt Morgan has an excellent blog post about&nbsp;
this called testing the async cloud with AWS CDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:32.160
The CDK book also has a section on this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:41.760
Another advanced topic for the CDK are aspects&nbsp;
aspects are a very powerful tool in CDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.280 --> 00:51:47.040
There are a way to apply an operation&nbsp;
to all constructs in a given scope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:53.600
The aspect could modify the constructs such as by&nbsp;
adding tags, or it could verify something or about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.600 --> 00:51:57.680
the state of the constructs such as&nbsp;
ensuring that all the buckets are encrypted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:59.760 --> 00:52:05.760
During the Prepare phase CDK calls the visit&nbsp;
method of the object for the construct and each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:12.160
its children in a top down order. The visit method&nbsp;
is free to change anything in the construct.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:19.520
In this example, the bucket version checking&nbsp;
class implements a visit method that checks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:25.280
if s3 bucket versioning is enabled. Here it&nbsp;
throws an error, but you could just as easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:31.440
modify the construct to enable versioning.&nbsp;
Everything that the stack is applied to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:37.520
that is every construct within the stack&nbsp;
is evaluated by this visit method. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:43.040
is why we need to check the instance of the node&nbsp;
being evaluated to make sure it's the CFN bucket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:49.360
The nodes will all end up being the level&nbsp;
one construct of the resource in question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:52.560
Even if you use a level two bucket&nbsp;
construct within your stack code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.320 --> 00:53:01.760
For best practices, AWS breaks up their&nbsp;
best practice recommendations into four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.760 --> 00:53:07.760
different areas. Organization&nbsp;
coding construct an application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:10.160 --> 00:53:14.240
For organization best practices,&nbsp;
AWS recommends having a team of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:14.240 --> 00:53:19.680
CDK experts help set standards and train&nbsp;
or mentor developers in the use of CDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:24.320
This doesn't have to be a large team. It&nbsp;
could be as small as one or two people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:27.840
or it could be a center of excellence&nbsp;
if you have a large organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.080 --> 00:53:35.600
AWS also recommends the practice of deploying&nbsp;
to multiple AWS accounts. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:35.600 --> 00:53:42.080
having separate production, QA and development&nbsp;
accounts. You should use continuous integration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.080 --> 00:53:47.360
and continuous deployment tools like CDK pipelines&nbsp;
for deploying beyond development accounts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:50.080 --> 00:53:55.920
For coding best practices, AWS recommends to&nbsp;
only add complexity where you actually need it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:56.720 --> 00:54:02.160
Don't architect for all possible scenarios up&nbsp;
front. So the keep it simple, stupid principle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:10.080
It also recommends to use the&nbsp;
well architected framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:10.080 --> 00:54:15.440
AWS CDK apps are a mechanism to codify and&nbsp;
deliver well architected best practices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.240 --> 00:54:24.400
As you follow these principles, you can create&nbsp;
and share components that implement them. Earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:24.400 --> 00:54:29.920
I stated it's possible to have multiple apps in a&nbsp;
project, which is true, but it's not recommended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:39.600
Best practice is to not do that and to only have a&nbsp;
single app per repo. In a CI CD world changes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:44.480
one app could unnecessarily trigger the deployment&nbsp;
of another app, even if it didn't change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:50.880
Finally, your CDK code and the code&nbsp;
that implements your runtime logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:56.480
should be in the same package. They don't need&nbsp;
to live in separate repositories or packages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:05.840
For construct best practices, you should be&nbsp;
building constructs of your logical units of code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.400 --> 00:55:10.880
If you consistently build websites with&nbsp;
s3, buckets, API gateways and lambdas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:11.600 --> 00:55:14.480
make that a construct and then&nbsp;
use the construct in your stack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.880 --> 00:55:20.240
It's a bad practice to use environment&nbsp;
variable lookups within your constructs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:25.280
environment variable lookups should&nbsp;
happen at the top level of a CDK app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:25.280 --> 00:55:33.040
and then be passed to the stacks and constructs as&nbsp;
properties. By using environment variable lookups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:33.040 --> 00:55:37.200
and constructs you lose portability and&nbsp;
make your constructs harder to reuse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.920 --> 00:55:45.120
If you avoid network lookups during synthesis&nbsp;
and model all your production stages in code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:50.000
you can run a full suite of unit tests at&nbsp;
build time consistently in all environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.960 --> 00:55:57.920
Be careful how you fact refactor your code&nbsp;
that may result in changes to the logical ID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.880 --> 00:56:04.160
The logical ID is derived from the ID you&nbsp;
specify when you instantiate a construct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:04.160 --> 00:56:11.760
and the constructs position in the app tree.&nbsp;
Changing the logical idea of a resource results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:11.760 --> 00:56:16.800
in the resource being replaced with a new one at&nbsp;
the next deployment, which you almost never want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:18.880 --> 00:56:22.320
constructs are great, but they're&nbsp;
not built to enforce compliance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:23.680 --> 00:56:29.840
Compliance is better suited to using Service&nbsp;
control policies and permission boundaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.000 --> 00:56:39.680
For application best practices, make your&nbsp;
decisions at synthesis time. Don't use cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:44.960
formation conditions and parameters. For&nbsp;
example, a common CDK practice of iterating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:51.280
over a list and instantiating a construct for each&nbsp;
isn't possible using CloudFormation expressions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:57.840
Treat CloudFormation as an implementation&nbsp;
detail and not a language target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:02.400
You're not writing CloudFormation templates,&nbsp;
you're writing code that happens to generate them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:08.480
If you leave out resource names, CDK will generate&nbsp;
them for you. And it will do so in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.480 --> 00:57:14.640
won't cause problems when you end up refactoring&nbsp;
your code later. CDK does its best to keep you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:19.840
from losing data by defaulting to policies that&nbsp;
retain everything you create. But Cloud watch is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.840 --> 00:57:26.720
expensive. Go through and set your own retention&nbsp;
policies. CDK also has helper methods that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:31.920
empty an s3 bucket using a custom resource prior&nbsp;
to destroying a stack which is very useful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:35.040 --> 00:57:42.720
Consider keeping stateful resources like databases&nbsp;
in a separate stack from stateless resources. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:47.520
can turn on termination protection for stateful&nbsp;
stacks, while keeping it off for stateless once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.120 --> 00:57:53.200
Stateful resources are more sensitive to&nbsp;
construct renaming, which is another reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:53.200 --> 00:57:58.760
to keep them in a separate stack. It reduces&nbsp;
risk when reorganizing your app later. Determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.520 --> 00:58:07.040
determinism is key to successful&nbsp;
CDK deployments. CDK context is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.040 --> 00:58:11.920
mechanism for recording a snapshot of the non&nbsp;
deterministic values coming from synthesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.560 --> 00:58:20.405
This allows future synthesis operation&nbsp;
to produce the same exact template. non&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.405 --> 00:58:26.320
deterministic values results in using things like&nbsp;
a constructs from lookup where you're looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.320 --> 00:58:36.080
up an external resource. These from LOOKUP&nbsp;
values are cached in CDK context dot JSON.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.080 --> 00:58:40.800
The grant convenience methods allow you&nbsp;
to greatly simplify the IAM process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:46.320
Manually defining roles or using&nbsp;
predefined roles in your AWS account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:50.240
causes a big loss and flexibility&nbsp;
in how you design your applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:57.280
Service control policies and permission boundaries&nbsp;
are better alternatives to predefined roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:03.840
Another CDK best practice is to&nbsp;
create a stack for each environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:05.680 --> 00:59:10.960
When you synthesize your application, the cloud&nbsp;
assembly created in the CDK dot out folder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:17.840
contains a separate template for each&nbsp;
environment. Your entire build is deterministic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.280 --> 00:59:24.640
Most of the level two constructs in CDK have&nbsp;
convenience methods to help in the creation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:31.840
metrics, which you can easily use to generate&nbsp;
CloudWatch dashboards and alarms use them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

